<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuscarora people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tuscarora Nation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuscarora are an Iroquoian people originally from the coastal plain of North Carolina. After their defeat by colonists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuscarora-war">Tuscarora War</a> of 1711–1713, most moved north and around 1722 were adopted into the Iroquois Confederacy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> sponsorship, which then became the Six Nations. Many supported the Americans in the Revolution, and today they live on the Tuscarora Reservation near Niagara Falls and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations of the Grand River</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuscarora-people.jpg" alt="Tuscarora people" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Catlin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Catlin_-_C%C3%BA-sick,_Son_of_the_Chief_-_1985.66.271_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tuscarora are an Iroquoian people of the Northeastern Woodlands. Their own name, Skarù·ręˀ, may mean &quot;hemp gatherers&quot; or &quot;shirt-wearing people&quot;. Before European contact they lived in the Carolinas along the Roanoke, Neuse, Tar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tar-river">Pamlico Rivers</a>, as a confederacy of three tribes, the Katenuaka, Akawenteaka and Skaruren. Their members belonged to about seven matrilineal clans: Bear, Beaver, Deer, Eel, Snipe, Turtle and Wolf. Their language, Skarure, belongs to the northern branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iroquoian-languages">Iroquoian languages</a> and is closely related to Nottoway and Meherrin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In early 18th-century North Carolina, colonists reported two main branches of the Tuscarora, a northern group led by Tom Blunt and a southern group led by Hancock. Hancock's people suffered colonists who raided villages and kidnapped people to sell into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, as well as epidemic disease. On 22 September 1711 the southern Tuscarora and allied nations attacked settlers, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuscarora-war">Tuscarora War</a>.</p><p>North Carolina's governor secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>'s help, and in 1713 the southern Tuscarora were defeated at Fort Neoheroka, where 900 were killed or captured. Hancock was captured with Blunt's help and executed. About 1,500 Tuscarora fled north, and in 1722, sponsored by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a>, the Tuscarora were accepted as the sixth nation of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy. Migration north continued over about 90 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, part of the Tuscarora allied with the colonists, while those who supported the British followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, where they helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations of the Grand River</a> reserve. In the War of 1812 Tuscarora warriors fought a delaying action at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewiston-new-york">Lewiston, New York</a>, on 19 December 1813, allowing townspeople to escape.</p><p>Today the Tuscarora Nation lives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, near Niagara Falls, and the Tuscarora First Nation is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations of the Grand River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. In the mid-20th century New York City commissioner Robert Moses negotiated the purchase of 550 acres of the reservation for the reservoir of a Niagara hydroelectric project, a controversial sale.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tuscarora people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the <b>Tuscarora</b> moved north from North Carolina after the Tuscarora War of 1711–1713 and were adopted around 1722, they joined under the sponsorship of the Oneida, and the league became the Six Nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Today the <b>Tuscarora Nation</b> lives near Lewiston, New York, and at Six Nations of the Grand River in Ontario.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuscarora-nation">Tuscarora Nation</a></li></ul></section>
